#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124276 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "253b765d-be85-574e-8e32-c359ffbecdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3694" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+6{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }i+{e}^{t}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536170 :question-generator/id #uuid "dc058c52-7148-5c08-8526-74b5e128c3e4" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186124469.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d91284b-aa86-5efd-8e00-bc13b952dcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321627} {:answer/id #uuid "57674ee3-9965-5502-a818-bbcc31f0235a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321835} {:answer/id #uuid "60879608-43b6-5c2d-8f50-f7877f002d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321904} {:answer/id #uuid "8305ad0c-8f86-50aa-a55a-367d449dabc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322180} {:answer/id #uuid "e18c50bc-afa0-58da-b693-f0e3601201ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60879608-43b6-5c2d-8f50-f7877f002d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321904} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71b9994-814b-5c24-af5b-f67cb4ffae0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ea55a51-bc91-526a-9277-7689869bc683" :question/math-academy-id "q-36211" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y={e}^{-t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i-{e}^{-t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i-{e}^{-t}\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d33325-0f7c-59d5-9622-3be484ea7f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445645} {:answer/id #uuid "28207432-84ed-53b8-96d3-f43fe1450b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445646} {:answer/id #uuid "683e544e-ffb7-547d-87ef-b5301c6dac41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{sec}⁡t}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445656} {:answer/id #uuid "99330b27-129d-50df-97de-421d0fe9607b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t+1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445661} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4236d2-abd7-5e33-9757-638b2e5b9cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28207432-84ed-53b8-96d3-f43fe1450b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445646} :answer-field/id #uuid "00f4d712-e22f-5cf5-b66e-67d08872e484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c45146-95bd-53af-b2f0-49b5ef23b12e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95056" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡xy$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡xy)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{xy}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y={t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1ec52e-ccdc-5b0f-b240-92720661c62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+9{t}^{3}+6{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321508} {:answer/id #uuid "5c35907b-6462-5098-94af-12ec14663f58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-3t-3t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321877} {:answer/id #uuid "82fd3b74-e5d5-5eb9-84d2-cb77ba0c33b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-3{t}^{2}+36{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322179} {:answer/id #uuid "ac96cf45-01f8-5f94-b48d-b88b9ae7af6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{2}-12{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322499} {:answer/id #uuid "d89116e6-d4ee-5b61-abf5-08714b411c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d89116e6-d4ee-5b61-abf5-08714b411c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842} :answer-field/id #uuid "d379d752-f7ce-576e-9786-94b4166ca354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1916a81-682a-57cf-b7a5-c43c18c456ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-37099" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2x-x{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2-{y}^{2})\\,i-2xy\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=3{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j) \\\\  & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0661fd29-a292-5ab4-b31a-2396be427221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357808} {:answer/id #uuid "2c19e560-7929-538c-bcc2-9884cff52e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358147} {:answer/id #uuid "bb354d15-c911-57ac-961b-2bf623e8c47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359378} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd453d2-7215-5f3b-8c26-6cee0b42f4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359383} {:answer/id #uuid "caaabb05-4b0e-56ee-9471-80ef684ce597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c19e560-7929-538c-bcc2-9884cff52e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358147} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c5c1098-6416-500c-946c-2648c90f4466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eac0cd5-9d30-54ff-89b8-2cfd1b9003d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95052" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $r(t)=\\pi t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\pi t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\pi \\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j)\\cdot (\\pi \\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\pi {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e56dc23-76eb-5db9-9fce-e4d36ef6a300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3({t}^{2}+1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358426} {:answer/id #uuid "50d39335-f934-5320-9bd6-5f69635a09d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}{e}^{t}+2}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358445} {:answer/id #uuid "7dab4a7a-ef04-5286-8145-c994f7fd0f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358847} {:answer/id #uuid "de80fcbe-829a-5dae-a81d-fb3cf700a555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t(t-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359706} {:answer/id #uuid "eb612ba8-e57f-520d-9b63-e9ab14d4e478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t{e}^{t}-1)}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dab4a7a-ef04-5286-8145-c994f7fd0f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358847} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed81ec3-d906-5a86-bc7c-31b1ba6834fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99c33c9d-4cdd-5e75-a91b-a13201898eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-95054" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={t}^{-2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{2}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{-2}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{-2}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{{e}^{t}}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{-2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =-2{t}^{-3}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2{t}^{-3}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =-2({t}^{2})({t}^{-3})+2{e}^{-t}({e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f3235b-60e2-5c23-a6d1-2912bfc76c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358041} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fdac84-5c94-5bcb-847f-c1a99a8c5411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359347} {:answer/id #uuid "bde855a4-7fc2-52c3-af6f-b2e1fd0d6c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359405} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8b8503-66f4-52dd-b74c-bee16b502323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359689} {:answer/id #uuid "e953648a-9a4f-5892-9fbd-2c82540d2fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde855a4-7fc2-52c3-af6f-b2e1fd0d6c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359405} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3efa02-f1ae-5df0-98fd-0df55d2c7698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d03333b-22a3-579a-b479-87085056a6c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95059" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c059460-c4c2-55a2-8585-7e51d8edae78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445644} {:answer/id #uuid "47522fd9-62bd-5474-804d-67ac7a770b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{sin}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445650} {:answer/id #uuid "4b40ea9e-cf34-5238-9134-0581b982e962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445651} {:answer/id #uuid "c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445665} {:answer/id #uuid "e85749fe-f788-582a-a8ba-6a6fb947faa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445665} :answer-field/id #uuid "47babdd0-5f4c-5c9a-9ce1-4d934697545f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "240fc49a-d022-5d2c-b6ad-58058500b98b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95051" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189f09ce-2bb2-5efc-97b0-05b2a4b3fbb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{t}+t}{t{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479398} {:answer/id #uuid "5b802ceb-0898-5764-b40e-d7ea6a72486c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479489} {:answer/id #uuid "629e7fba-04e6-54e1-8d56-6ef59c0da0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}+3}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479503} {:answer/id #uuid "65254441-20bf-539f-a01a-719a7b06e2d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t{e}^{t}+3}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479506} {:answer/id #uuid "b44d800e-4a42-5329-8b39-02e75b4ca367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t+3}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b44d800e-4a42-5329-8b39-02e75b4ca367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t+3}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479628} :answer-field/id #uuid "306c4e3a-7620-56d0-8ab5-267aba392210" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ad8b1a6-b8e1-5739-bfbc-4544e317c6e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-95055" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}y}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j)\\cdot (\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{t+3}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275babd6-f291-5f51-9ada-2d31636b381f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479417} {:answer/id #uuid "5e27ed02-56c4-5c23-9e9b-e839f9be174b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479496} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0375e9-56a8-5366-9c6f-1cacf6261aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479543} {:answer/id #uuid "ceaa9304-14fa-5f01-a4f8-01269d44b5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479676} {:answer/id #uuid "f416028e-1e7f-5522-8bc3-1c8b6b7f478d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceaa9304-14fa-5f01-a4f8-01269d44b5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479676} :answer-field/id #uuid "8891d227-4f4f-5b72-a172-ba46661cec18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "608efbbd-2dcf-58ab-9644-3b94a19193a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-167407" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy}$ and $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3}$ and $y=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2t}+\\frac{t}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e8a119-89e7-5e83-8ece-e6e69dba1106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t-2){e}^{{t}^{2}+t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479393} {:answer/id #uuid "571e0ae0-7b23-51f2-b237-5930ff5b4a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}+t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479484} {:answer/id #uuid "68fdb16f-c294-542e-b24f-3fcf75971b20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t{e}^{{t}^{2}+t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479515} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2b5ef5-5b34-543c-ac98-cc2b1b5278c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479637} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9e20ec-6a55-5371-95d9-8a1fc2f05681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "571e0ae0-7b23-51f2-b237-5930ff5b4a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}+t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479484} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c59d1a9-7623-5670-96a8-dca459663150" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa703c37-d0d3-5fc5-8021-cece98eb14f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167406" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={e}^{x-y}$ and $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=-t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,i-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i-\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,i-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,j)\\cdot (2t\\,i-\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\cdot 2t+(-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t})\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =(2t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032cc53a-d445-5e45-affb-dbc26bb23949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+3t-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479365} {:answer/id #uuid "38580c3a-82d9-598a-afe3-0eb7825be631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479443} {:answer/id #uuid "446c6267-5187-59df-b71e-b14bacbbc752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+4t-3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479459} {:answer/id #uuid "c876a545-69bd-57fe-b996-cb8971e263d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479663} {:answer/id #uuid "cb391fc8-c0cb-57ad-8f3d-ae952561b48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c876a545-69bd-57fe-b996-cb8971e263d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479663} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d24599-ab4c-5c66-8f82-5120438263f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5969d89-d7a6-55d3-97d8-1ebc95c98dd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95053" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+y)\\,i+x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1$ and $y=-{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2t+2-{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2t+2-{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\,i-2t\\,j) \\\\  & =2t+2-{t}^{2}-2t(t+1) \\\\  & =2t+2-{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}-2t \\\\  & =2-3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0618c56b-92f8-5743-9e32-328933507a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}+{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514887} {:answer/id #uuid "42831ed2-f44c-506e-a16d-0e06ead786c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}-{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515014} {:answer/id #uuid "581a6ccb-d929-5a4c-abd5-314717c764d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}+t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515068} {:answer/id #uuid "b182ff13-9dbc-5c38-8d5b-05e109460201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515243} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bd0e4e-b413-5783-9897-cec63d3177bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "581a6ccb-d929-5a4c-abd5-314717c764d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}+t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515068} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb54adf-64fe-5825-9ef0-62336c5f858a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2becf284-e142-5ae3-80f6-d208696ea202" :question/math-academy-id "q-95068" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}+t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09dbf362-0ea5-5f52-a5a9-966c3c7d12bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(2 e^{2 t} + \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536102} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fe6f8-cd4f-5ff8-9a88-a56b4315d98e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536122} {:answer/id #uuid "742a3fd6-b9c4-5147-a632-39b718c9a4c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536138} {:answer/id #uuid "9011cc4b-f8e1-5639-a6a0-59f04c5aa9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\left(- \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536148} {:answer/id #uuid "9a209fc2-c827-5e3f-995a-0fc60aebe583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\left(e^{4 t} + \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fe6f8-cd4f-5ff8-9a88-a56b4315d98e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536122} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c7be4e-6cd5-5ed1-a27a-c153bf398a09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cb8adf3-5059-5402-b886-e9c6e0d73f1e" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4xy}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}}{1+4({e}^{2t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4({e}^{2t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)}{1+4({e}^{2t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}+\\frac{16{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2t}(2\\sin^{2}⁡4t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a57213b-4fa3-5117-81e3-a2d1f931c7b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536103} {:answer/id #uuid "0f22572f-894b-53df-a836-434bde50cdac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 e^{3 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536105} {:answer/id #uuid "6cda11da-4b3d-5818-8261-a86fabce7b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\left(2 e^{3 t} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536137} {:answer/id #uuid "c38550f7-4952-57df-9ae9-77ce137fab61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(3 e^{6 t} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536162} {:answer/id #uuid "cd04a189-9235-59c8-a370-b182647b1c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd04a189-9235-59c8-a370-b182647b1c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536164} :answer-field/id #uuid "a578ac36-cb48-5e2b-b8a0-622f82cf0b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e7834dc-d7fc-52ab-b7ab-d76e42910958" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{3t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{3t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}}{1+({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2({e}^{3t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{1+({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j)\\cdot (3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3t}\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}+\\frac{4{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3t}(3\\sin^{2}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cded62a-f1b3-5de4-935c-37ceeec7974e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(8 e^{8 t} + 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536104} {:answer/id #uuid "155e3333-446f-5203-8b48-f118c022d0a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math}